                                Appendix II. Some special cases

      A simple Computer program shows that the only possible counter-examples to the
Jacobian conjecture for polynomials of degrees less than 100 are of degrees (64, 68),
(84, 56), (75, 50) and (99, 66) with some special data attached. A routine computation
shows that the numbers of coefficients of the pairs of polynomials involved are 3370,
5308, 4352 and 7348 respectively. The task of direct verification of the Jacobian con-
jecture by computation for those pairs of polynomials with the said degrees is beyond
the capacity of a large Computer. In this Appendix we shall illustrate some methods to
reduce the numbers of coefficients, and then settle the problems of computations.
